.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-webhooks-test - Send a test event to the webhook's endpoint and show what it answered


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 webhooks test  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Send one test event to the webhook's callback URL now, and print what the
endpoint did: delivered (a 2xx), the status code and the start of its response
body — or, when no response came, why (a refused address, TLS, a timeout).

.PP
The event has topic "webhooks.test", "test": true and no payment, and is signed
and delivered exactly like a real one, so it checks reachability, TLS and your
signature verification without waiting for a real payment. It is not logged in
\&'event-callbacks', does not count toward the circuit breaker, and works on a
disabled webhook too — test the endpoint before re-enabling it.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for test


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 webhooks test whk_abc123
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-webhooks(1)\fP
